     The ttaapp interface is a software loopback mechanism that can be loosely
     described as the network interface analog of the pty(4), that is, ttaapp
     does for network interfaces what the pty(4) driver does for terminals.

     The ttaapp driver, like the pty(4) driver, provides two interfaces: an in‐
     terface like the usual facility it is simulating (an Ethernet network in‐
     terface in the case of ttaapp, or a terminal for pty(4)), and a character-
     special device “control” interface.  A client program transfers Ethernet
     frames to or from the ttaapp “control” interface.  The tun(4) interface pro‐
     vides similar functionality at the network layer: a client will transfer
     IP (by default) packets to or from a tun(4) “control” interface.

     The network interfaces are named “tap0”, “tap1”, etc., one for each con‐
     trol device that has been opened.  These Ethernet network interfaces per‐
     sist until _i_f___t_u_n_t_a_p_._k_o module is unloaded, or until removed with "ifcon‐
     fig destroy" (see below).

     ttaapp devices are created using interface cloning.  This is done using the
     “ifconfig tapNN create” command.  This is the preferred method of creating
     ttaapp devices.  The same method allows removal of interfaces.  For this,
     use the “ifconfig tapNN destroy” command.

     If the sysctl(8) variable _n_e_t_._l_i_n_k_._t_a_p_._d_e_v_f_s___c_l_o_n_i_n_g is non-zero, the ttaapp
     interface permits opens on the special control device _/_d_e_v_/_t_a_p.  When
     this device is opened, ttaapp will return a handle for the lowest unused ttaapp
     device (use devname(3) to determine which).

